WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:07.710
SCOTT HAALAND: In the
next couple minutes,

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.830
I'll demonstrate how
easy it is to leverage

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:13.622
existing Apigee APIs to
develop no-code applications.

00:00:13.622 --> 00:00:15.830
We're going to start in the
Apigee environment, where

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:19.070
we have an open API
specification, an API

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:22.820
proxy that represents that
spec, and an API product, which

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:26.900
publishes the API proxy for
client applications to consume.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:30.500
Today an app created in
AppSheet will be our client.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.410
In our Hipster
Products API proxy,

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:37.470
we can see a pre-flow policy
that checks for an API key.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.010
This helps to secure
the API, as only

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:44.060
apps that have this secret
key can invoke the API.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:46.010
Next we can see the
API product that

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:48.620
publishes the API for
client applications

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:52.040
to use called Hipster Silver.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:54.190
At the bottom of the
API product definition,

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:56.990
we can see that an app has
been registered already

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:58.360
for AppSheet.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.610
And this is where we can
get the API key for our app.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:03.710
Now we're going to
switch personas.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:06.710
We've been an Apigee API
producer up until now.

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:10.340
In AppSheet, we will put on
the hat of Paul Parker, who's

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:12.560
a line-of-business user.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.690
The first thing we'll do
is create a data source

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:17.010
to point to our API in Apigee.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:21.590
We'll click on the Plus Data
Source to add a new one.

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:23.990
We'll name the data source
Apigee Hipsters Demo

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:26.150
and select the Apigee type.

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:28.850
We enter the API key
from Apigee and the URL

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:30.680
to the OpenAPI spec.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:33.470
And now we can validate
and then authorize access

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:35.730
to create the data source.

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:37.490
Next, we create an app.

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:39.440
We'll go to the My Apps area.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.840
AppSheet noticed that we
don't have any apps yet,

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.030
so it's assuming we
want to create one.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:45.290
We do.

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:47.690
We'll use the Start From
Your Own Data option,

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:50.910
and the data will come
from our Apigee API.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.210
We'll call this
app Hipsters App.

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:56.270
Now is when we get to choose the
Apigee data source we created.

00:01:56.270 --> 00:01:58.910
I'll select Apigee
Hipsters Demo.

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:00.460
Let's select Product.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:02.798
AppSheet just created
the app for us.

00:02:02.798 --> 00:02:05.090
And we can see the preview
of the app in the right pane

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:06.180
already.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:07.880
We just need to make
a few adjustments.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:11.410
I know that there is a picture
of each product in the data,

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:13.150
and that's not showing up.

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:16.640
We'll change the
picture type to Image.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:18.110
The product ID is kind of ugly.

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:20.870
Let's change the app
to not show that.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:23.240
And then it will show
the product name first.

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:24.823
Let's save our changes.

00:02:24.823 --> 00:02:26.990
Now the images are showing
up, and the product names

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:28.130
look great.

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:31.062
AppSheet helped us create our
first app within a minute.

00:02:31.062 --> 00:02:33.020
Let's go to the Tablet
view so that you can see

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:34.730
a larger version of the app.

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:37.640
We can see that there's
also a Detail page created

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.000
for each of the products.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.880
And we can navigate
from product to product

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:42.420
on the Detail screen.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:44.330
Now let's go back
to the summary list.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:47.770
Once I've adjusted the app to
be just how I want it to be,

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:50.210
I'll be able to deploy
it to mobile devices

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:51.840
and as a web app.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:55.010
This was a quick overview of how
to leverage your existing APIs

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:56.900
to build no-code applications.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:59.030
Are you ready to empower
your business users

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:01.480
with AppSheet and Apigee?

